\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}
\newtheorem{corollary}{Corollary}

If traces are unfaithful \citep{turpin2024language}, the mirage is inherent to extraction. Three testable predictions follow: (1)~BR $> 1.0$ across architectures (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}); (2)~no same-model intervention reduces BR below $1.0$ (\S\ref{sec:remediation}); (3)~only external verification escapes the mirage (\S\ref{sec:cross-arch}).

\begin{proposition}[Self-Extraction Ceiling]\label{prop:ceiling}
For generator $M$ with $\text{BR}(M) > 1$ and oracle improvement $\Delta_\text{oracle}$, self-extracted constraints improve SC by at most $\Delta_\text{max}^\text{self} \lesssim \Delta_\text{oracle} / \text{BR}(M)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[Capability Gap Condition]\label{cor:capability_gap}
Let $G$ be a generator with SC accuracy $\text{SC}_G$ and let $V$ be an external verifier with standalone accuracy $V_\text{acc}$ and inter-system correlation $\rho_{GV} < \rho_{GG}$. Combo gain $\Delta_\text{combo} - \Delta_\text{SC}$ is monotonically increasing in the capability gap $(V_\text{acc} - \text{SC}_G)$.
\end{corollary}

\textit{Proof sketch.} From Proposition~\ref{prop:ceiling}, the SC ceiling is bounded by $\text{BR}$. Adding $V$ with $\rho_{GV} < \rho_{GG}$ increases effective independent signals: $K_\text{eff}^\text{combo} = K_\text{eff}^\text{SC} + w_V \cdot (1 - \rho_{GV})/(1 - \rho_{GG})$, reducing the effective bias ratio. However, when $V_\text{acc} < \text{SC}_G$, incorrect verifier votes counteract independence gains. The net effect follows a sigmoid in the capability gap. Empirically, our cross-architecture evaluation yields Spearman $\rho = 0.970$ ($p < 0.001$) with sigmoid $R^2 = 0.875$. $\square$

