\paragraph{Implications for trained reasoning.}
Our findings illuminate why the paradigm shift from test-time aggregation to trained reasoning internalization (e.g., o1, DeepSeek-R1, Qwen3 thinking mode) yields outsized gains. Qwen3-14B in thinking mode achieves $86\%$ on FOLIO, already exceeding the theoretical self-extraction ceiling of Mistral-7B (${\sim}62\%$; Proposition~\ref{prop:ceiling} with $\text{BR} = 4.97$). Rather than extracting and aggregating external symbolic structure---which inherits the generator's biases---these models internalize verification during training, bypassing the independence bottleneck entirely. Corollary~\ref{cor:capability_gap} formalizes this boundary: test-time aggregation helps only when verifier capability exceeds generator capability, a condition that trained reasoning renders unnecessary.

